--[[For Q-SYS Designer, the best approach would be a Q-SYS Plugin (Lua-based) that interfaces with the Speaksee/SoundShape system rather than a schematic-design plugin. Q-SYS plugins are used to create control components, manage APIs, monitor status, and automate AV workflows.

Based on the available Speaksee documentation, the hardware uses Wi‑Fi/Ethernet networking and is managed through software services, making it suitable for a networked Q‑SYS integration.

Proposed Q-SYS Plugin Features
Control Functions
Start/Stop transcription
Enable/Disable translation
Select language
Mute microphones
Microphone battery monitoring
Connection status monitoring
Monitoring
Online/Offline state
Active speaker count
Audio input status
Translation status
Network connectivity
UCI Controls
+---------------------------------+
| Speaksee Controller             |
+---------------------------------+
| Status: Connected               |
| Language: English → Dutch       |
| Participants: 3                 |
|                                 |
| [Start] [Stop]                  |
| [Translate On/Off]              |
|                                 |
| Mic1 Battery 92%                |
| Mic2 Battery 88%                |
| Mic3 Battery 97%                |
+---------------------------------+
]]-- 
Basic Q-SYS Plugin Skeleton
PluginInfo = {
    Name = "Speaksee Controller",
    Version = "1.0",
    Id = "com.soundshape.speaksee"
}

Controls = {
    {
        Name = "Connect",
        ControlType = "Button"
    },
    {
        Name = "Status",
        ControlType = "Indicator"
    },
    {
        Name = "Language",
        ControlType = "Text"
    }
}

--HTTP API Example

--If Speaksee exposes REST endpoints:

HttpClient.Get {
    Url = "http://SPEAKSEE-IP/api/status",
    EventHandler = function(tbl, code, data)
        if code == 200 then
            Controls.Status.Boolean = true
        end
    end
}

